strip_ckpt.py against Team 7's export_safetensors(), on a commit with dry run 2's parameter split (#869:
176,398,881 parameters, 165,740,544 with one optimizer buffer each), saved by Team 7's Checkpointer.

Where: Linux (Docker python:3.11-slim on a 4-CPU, 6 GB VM), torch 2.15.0+cpu, 4 threads. The commit is read right
after it is written, so the page cache is warm: the times are CPU and memory times, and "Read" counts bytes through
read() (rchar), from the cache or the disk. On a network store the read bytes set the time.
The probe bits are saved for a GPU device class ('cuda:NVIDIA H100 80GB HBM3'), as in a GPU-trained run: the export
runs the CPU probe but cannot compare it. One SGD-momentum optimizer keeps one buffer per parameter, so the commit is
about 3% smaller than dry run 2's would be (no second AdamW buffer on 10.7M parameters). The tensor count (192) is
not the run's. For --verify fingerprints the build adds MANIFEST.json fingerprints of the model.* tensors, made with
Team 7's own fingerprints(), as Team 7's save would keep them (a proposal: state_digest() computes them already).

Command (each path runs in a fresh process):
  python teams/09-evaluation/harness_lab/tests/bench_strip.py --work /tmp/b1 --scale 1
  python teams/09-evaluation/harness_lab/tests/bench_strip.py --work /tmp/b0.1 --scale 0.1

Checks in the same state: harness_lab/tests on Linux, 88 passed (test_strip_ckpt.py: 12).

